1 NOTE {geni:about_me} III. Adam Leijel, d. ä., född 1623, död 1686, Patrik Lyells tredje son, blef  handelsman i Stockholm. Gift i början af 1660-talet med Helena Radou, född  i början af 1640-talet, död 1701. Hennes föräldrar voro hand
2 CONC elsmannen i  Stockholm och bruksegaren Adam Radou, född omkring 1610, död 1687 och hans  första maka, född Dress, dotter till ofvannämnde kronans arrendator af Nora  och Lindes bergslager Anders Dress, född 1652, och Elisabeth Sch
2 CONC aeij, död  1673. Adam Radous fader Jacob Radu hade inkommit från Skottland till  Stockholm, der han blef en framstående köpman. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Af Adam Leijels d. ä. barn äro fem döttrar, alla gifta, med visshet kända,  nämligen: 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Eva''', död 1705, gift första gången 1682 med bankokommissarien Lars  Nyman, andra gången 1695 med notarien i Bergskollegium Nils Perman, död 1712,  hvars förste kände stamfader lefde i början af 1600-talet och var bonde i Pern
2 CONC o  by i Finland, efter hvilken släktnamnet Perman togs. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Magdalena''', gift 1688 med handelsmannen i Stockholm Jacob von Schoting,  född 1661, tillhörande en ätt från Flandern, som inkom till Sverige med Rolof  von Schoting, död 1643, rådman i Göteborg. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Barbara''', gift 1691 med handelsmannen i Stockholm, öfverinspektören Johan  Laurin, född 1663, död 1724, 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Judith''', gift 1707 med landshöfdingen i St. Kopparbergs län, presidenten  i Reduktionskommittén i Pommern Albrekt Lindberg, nobil. Lindercreutz.  född 1674, död 1744, och slöt sjelf sin adliga ätt, samt 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Catarina''', gift med handelsmannen i Karlshamn Christopher Cuur. 
2 CONT
2 CONT En son, eller snarare kanske sonson, till Adam Leijel d. ä, torde vara  löjtnanten vid generalmajoren, friherre Kruses Cavallerie-Regemente Carl Leijel,  hvilken blef med sitt regemente fången vid Dnieprströmmen efter slaget vid  
2 CONC Pultava samt förd till Tobolsk i Sibirien, der han blef ihjälslagen under ett  pöbelupplopp den 29 april 1712, som plötsligt uppstod, under det att en  förhärjande eldsvåda ödelade staden. De svenska fångarne höllo just på att  fr
2 CONC amgångsrikt dämpa eldens framfart, då de oförmodadt öfverföllos af en ursinnig  beväpnad pöbelhop, hvilken af illvilliga uppviglare intalats, att det just var  svenskarne, som påtändt staden för att mörda och plundra dess invånare
2 CONC . Ett  tjugutal officerare satte här lifvet till. 
2 CONT
2 CONT http://runeberg.org/strokirk/3/0194.html James Lyell (known as Jacob Leijel) was born in Arbroath, Scotland on 10/09/1612, the son of Patrick. He arrived in Sweden in 1638 with his brothers Adam, David and Henry. In 1639 on 27 Apr
2 CONC il, James became a burgess of Stockholm. The previous year, on 21 December, he sought citizenship for his servant James Reiff (Feiff?). It was apparently as a result of his second marriage that James came to own some ironworks. Th
2 CONC e brothers became important factory owners in Alvkarled, Rockhammar, Gottbohl (in Ununge Parish) and Harnas. James also had some financial transactions with the Momma-Reenstierna family in Sweden. The brothers also engaged in the 
2 CONC Swedish iron trade, Jacob exporting 55,290 shipounds and David 12,706 shipounds between 1651-60, making them together the third largest exporter of the commodity from Sweden (behind the Momma-Reenstierna brothers and Abraham Bex).
2 CONC  They also co-operated with Adam Raddou, the forth largest exporter who was Jacob Leijel's brother-in-law (adding his exports, the kin group of Leijel and Raddou exported the largest amount of iron, 130,000 ship pounds). The famil
2 CONC y partership also saw Raddou and Jacob lease all the Crown's forges and mines in the district of Nora and Linde during the 1660s. In the period between 1661-70, Jacob exported 33,465 ship pounds while David exported 21,980 ship po
2 CONC unds worth of iron, placing them above the Momma-Reenstierna brothers once more, highlighting their status within the iron export market. By 1674, James served as one of the 48 elders (äldste) of the city of Stockholm. In a letter
2 CONC  from Jacob Fahlgreen to Jacob David on 26 June 1677, Leijel was noted as working with 'Callendar' and employing a factor called John Harwitz. That man shipped 300 pieces of copper money plate to London for Leijel on board the shi
2 CONC p of Captain Heil (Englishman). Jacob got his birthbrieve from Scotland on 15/03/1655. He raised a case against James Feiff on 14 September 1661. In 1688, he received a tax free house "skattehemman" in Knutby parish, Stockholms lä
2 CONC n. Leijel married Margaretha Eden (1627-1653) in Stockholm in January 1644 and had two daughters, Gertrude (b.7 May 1645) and Margareta (b. 25 March 1647), a son Jacob (b.19 March 1649), and two further daughters, Eva Maria (b. 12
2 CONC  July 1650) and Catharina (b.13 August 1651). In 1653 he is listed as one of the 25 shareholders of the Swedish Moscow Company trading in the Baltic to Archangel and Reval. After the death of his first wife he remarried to Barbara
2 CONC  Maria Dress (1632-1694) whose father Andre came from France and mother, Elisabet Schaeij came from the Dutch Republic. James and Barbara had a son, Adam who was ennobled in 1719 and a daughter Maria. After Leijel's death in 1678,
2 CONC  he was buried in the St Nicholas church in Stockholm around 18 August, his widow Barbera took over the running of the Rockhammar factory. For Raddou to have been Leijel's brother-in-law, he must have married a sister of Jacob, or
2 CONC  there was another wife, or one of the names of the wives is not complete. It should be noted that an important member of the Eastland Company was James sr's brother, Adam, who became a member in 1666 in London. Jakob Patriksson L
2 CONC eijel was born 10 SEP 1612 in Aberbrothock, Skottland, and died 11 JUL 1678 in Stockholm. He married Margareta Klaesdotter Eden 1 JAN 1644, daughter of Klas Eden Edenberg and Gertrud Sulchen. She was born 18 APR 1627 in Uppsala,C,
2 CONC  and died 13 DEC 1653 in Nikolai,A. He married Barbara Maria Dress 11 MAR 1655, daughter of Andry Dress and Elisabet Schaeij. She was born 7 JAN 1632 in Gyttorp, Nora,T, and died 14 JUL 1694 in Nikolai,A. In December 1641 Jacob Ly
2 CONC ell bought in Stockholm several hundred pounds of tobacco which had been landed in Göteborg* from New Sweden, the Swedish colony on the Delaware, by the ship Kalmar Nykel in the spring of 1640. (see "The Swedish Settlements on the
2 CONC  Delaware, 1638-1664" by Dr. Amandus Johnson Vol 1 page 159, published by University of Pennsylvania, 1911.)
2 CONT * Where the cargo was placed in the care of Timon von Schotting, whose grandson Jacob von Schotting married the niece of Jacob Lyell, Magdalena Adamsdotter Leijel, 46 years later!
2 CONT
2 CONT  ........................................................................... Swedish Riksarkiv, Förteckning över Momma-Reenstierna Samlingen, part C: brev till Bröderna Momma-Reenstierna ingångna skrivelser, section 3, Affärer och
2 CONC  processer med nedan nämda enskilda personer, E2595, 127, no date; Swedish Riksarkiv, Bergskollegii skrivelser till Kung. Maj:t. 1640-1840 - Jakob Leijel 1665; Swedish Riksarkiv, Landshövdingarnas Skrivelser till Kung. Maj:t. Upla
2 CONC nds Län - Jacob Leijel, Bruksförvaltare i Knutby sn, Stockholms län, 11/06/1688, 21/10/1690; Stockholm Stadsarkiv: Borgare i Stockholm, Register 1601-1650, p.45; C. Dalhede, Handelsfamiljer pa Stormaktstidens Europamarknad (3 vols
2 CONC , Partille, 2001), II, p.420; Svenska Adelns Ättartavlor, vol. 4, p.521; Steve Murdoch, Network North: Scottish Kin, Commercial and Covert Associations in Northern Europe, 1603-1746 (Brill, Leiden, 2006), pp.181-182; Carl von Linn
2 CONC é 'Nemesis Divina' (1770), p.287. We thank Peter Leyel for this last reference.
