1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ÄTTEN LEIJEL / LYELL FRÅN SKOTTLAND. Ätten Leijel kom från Skottland, där deras förfader Adam Lyell var amtman i Aberbrothock. Släkten kommer av ätten Lyell of Murthill. John Lyell kom till Skottland från Northumbe
2 CONC rland år 1280. Hans son Hew Lyell ärvde landområdena kring Murthill och Kinalty av sin morfar, Malcom Ramsay of Auchterhouse. Arvet blev bekräftad av kung Robert II av Skottland år 1375/ 77.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ätten härstammar från de Lisle som kom att uttalas och skrivas Lyel eller Lyell (ev. Lyall). Ättens stamgods var slottet Duchat/ Duchal från vilket stamfadern styrde ättens angelägenheter. Stamätten kom sedan att bli den adlade ät
2 CONC ten Lyle. Baldwin de Lisle anses vara ättens stamfader.
2 CONT
2 CONT Walter Lyall, hereditary Town Clerk of Montrose, son of James Lyall of Balmaleedie, was born in 1595, and was the founder of the family of Lyall of Dysart and Gardyne. The Lyall name is pretty common in Forfarshire and Kincardines
2 CONC hire at the present time. It does not figure much in history, but is one of great respectability.
2 CONT
2 CONT There were two distinct and different families of Lyell/ Lyall in Scotland: 1) Lyalls of Duchal, in Renfrewshire, and 2) Lyells of Murthill, in Forfarshire.
2 CONT
2 CONT As both families originally came from England, they are believed to be descended from one common ancestor, Baldwin de Lisle. The Renfrew family settled in Scotland at the end of the eleventh century.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Forfarshire family came from Northumberland about 1280. John Lyell, who was the Forester of Plater, was the first of the family who settled in Forfarshire. He was the son of John Lyell de Blado, Episcopi de St. Andree. He marr
2 CONC ies Catherine, daughter of Ramsay of Auchterhouse, and by her had a son, Hew, who received a charter (kungabrev) of the lands of Murthill and Kinalty from Malcolm Ramsay of Auchterhouse. This charter was confirmed by Robert 11. in
2 CONC  1375.
2 CONT
2 CONT The decendants of Hew Lyall held Murthill till 1685, when the family became extinct on the death of Alexander Lyell. The chief branches of this family were: 1) Lyell of Woodhead, 2) Lyell of Blackness, 3) Lyell of Dunnichen and Ga
2 CONC rdyne, 4) Lyell of Balnagarro, Balmaleedie and Dysart.
2 CONT
2 CONT The first three of these branches have now become extinct. But Thomas Lyell of Dysart acquired Gardyne from John Lyell of Gardyne.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Lyells of Murthill and Balnagarro, in the middle of the sixteenth century were conspicuous for their unneighbourly feeling. They were continually at feud with the Ogilvies of Inshewn and the Provost and Bailies of Forfar, till
2 CONC  in 1589 they were bound over in 1000 marks each by the Privy Council to keep the peace.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT * Reference: [https://www.myheritage.com/research/record-1-179059442-1-512223/adam-lyell-of-murthill-in-myheritage-family-trees?indId=externalindividual-0cc10a0a85da91b10ecae0eb3ea9eebe&mrid=c9ce457444d302fb5ac259f2f45155c5 MyHeri
2 CONC tage Family Trees] - [https://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Mar 16 2018, 16:28:36 UTC''
