1 NOTE {geni:about_me} [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charlotta_Wers%C3%A4ll '''Eva Charlotta Wersäll'''] (née Lewenhaupt) was a Swedish noblewoman, a daughter of Count Carl Gustaf Lewenhaupt and Charlotta Elisabet von Essen. She had a s
2 CONC ister Charlotta Ulrika and three brothers, Erik, Carl Axel and Reinhold Abraham. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1879 she married Claës Wersäll, the future Finance Minister of Sweden. Among their 11 children, 9 were boys, one was a girl, and one died soon after birth. Six of the boys took part in the 1912 Summer Olympics: two as competito
2 CONC rs, one as an official, and three as assistants, while another one, Ture, won an Olympic gold medal earlier in 1906. For this unusual achievement, their mother was awarded a special gold medal at the 1912 Games.
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://sv.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charlotta_Wers%C3%A4ll '''Charlotte Wersäll'''], som ingick äktenskap 1879, födde barn ungefär vartannat år mellan 1881 och 1900. Det blev elva barn, varav tio nådde vuxen ålder. Nio av barnen var sö
2 CONC ner. Sex av sönerna deltog i de olympiska spelen 1912. Och det var alltså därför deras mor Charlotte Wersäll tilldelades den internationella olympiska kommitténs särskilda guldmedalj. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Källa: [http://www.stockholmskallan.se/ContentFiles/SSA/Biblioteket/Wersall_for_web.pdf Dagens Nyheter] 18 september 1912.
